<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Nuremberg</h1><p class="st-meta">1219 CE – 1806 CE · capital Nuremberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Franconian imperial city of Dürer and Hans Sachs, keeper of the imperial regalia and one of the great trading and craft centres of early modern Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> grew around an imperial castle first recorded in 1050 on a sandstone ridge above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pegnitz">Pegnitz</a>. Its merchants gained privileges from the Salian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> emperors, and in 1219 Frederick II's Great Charter of Freedom laid the basis for its self-government, gradually freeing it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollern</a> burgraves who held the castle.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A closed circle of patrician families ran the council, excluding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> from power. Metalwork, instruments, weapons and long-distance trade made the city rich, and from 1424 to 1796 it kept the imperial regalia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer">Albrecht Dürer</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veit-stoss">Veit Stoss</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-sachs">Hans Sachs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-behaim">Martin Behaim</a>, maker of the oldest surviving globe, worked there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> turned Lutheran in 1525 and won a large rural territory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-landshut-succession">Landshut War</a> of 1504 to 1505.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>War, debts and shifting trade routes weakened the city after 1600. In 1806 it was annexed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria</a>. It became a major industrial centre, and its old town was largely destroyed in 1945 and rebuilt.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Nuremberg" width="320" height="254">